PART 1 — CORE ACCOUNT ARCHITECTURE

1. Mother Account

Every Looma user has one mother account rooted in Firebase Authentication.

The authoritative authentication object is the existing Firebase user, such as "store.firebaseUser".

It controls:

- Firebase UID.
- Email address.
- Email verification.
- Authentication providers.
- Account security.
- Account recovery.
- Session management.
- Profile creation.
- Profile management.
- Profile switching.
- Global account preferences.

Do not create a separate Firebase account when a user creates a Business Profile.

A user can own or manage multiple Business Profiles using the same mother account.

The mother account is the authentication and account-management authority. It does not automatically acquire unrestricted authority over every profile-specific action.

Profile-specific operations must still pass the appropriate authorization checks.

2. Personal Profile

The personal profile is stored at:

"/users/{uid}"

Use the existing "UserProfile" type in "src/types/social.ts".

The personal profile is the human social identity.

It contains personal information such as:

- Immutable personal profile ID, associated with the Firebase UID.
- Display name.
- Independent username/handle.
- Avatar URL.
- Biography.
- Location.
- Interests.
- Personal followers and following.
- Personal social connections.
- Personal privacy settings.
- Personal posts and activity.

Personal settings must remain personal.

The personal profile must not become a business profile.

3. Business Profile Entity

Business Profiles are separate organizational entities stored at:

"/businessProfiles/{businessProfileId}"

Use the existing "BusinessProfile" type in "src/types/business.ts".

Each Business Profile must have:

- Immutable Business Profile ID.
- Independent business name.
- Independent username/slug.
- Business logo/avatar.
- Cover image.
- Biography.
- Business category.
- Website.
- Contact information.
- Business followers and following.
- Business-specific settings.
- Verification status.
- Commerce linkage.
- Store ID.
- Store slug.
- Business activity.

Business profile handles must be independently reserved in:

"/businessHandles/{slug}"

Normal and Business Profile usernames must not overwrite one another.

Changing a Business Profile name or username must not modify the personal profile.

4. Business Membership Relationship

Use:

"/businessMemberships/{membershipId}"

The relationship must identify:

- "userId"
- "businessProfileId"
- "role"
- "status"
- Creation timestamp.
- Relevant membership metadata.

Supported roles:

- OWNER
- ADMIN
- EDITOR
- VIEWER

A mother account can belong to multiple businesses.

A business can have multiple authorized members.

All business operations must verify the user's active membership and permissions.

Do not rely on frontend role checks alone.

5. Eliminate the Incorrect Account-Type Model

The initial audit identified an incorrect architectural approach involving fields such as:

- "accountType: 'personal' | 'business'"
- "businessCategory"
- "businessWebsiteUrl"

inside "/users/{uid}".

This conflates an individual human profile with an organizational entity.

Required correction:

- "/users/{uid}" remains the personal profile.
- "/businessProfiles/{bpId}" remains the business entity.
- "/businessMemberships/{id}" remains the relationship and authorization mechanism.
- Deprecate and ignore legacy "accountType: 'business'" for platform identity decisions.
- Remove the obsolete Activate Seller Store conversion workflow after verifying its dependencies.
- Preserve any existing business information that needs migration.
- Do not delete legacy fields until a safe migration and compatibility period have been completed.

Do not create a second competing identity architecture.

Use the existing Business Profile entity as the canonical business identity.

